(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-viral-and-fungal-infections-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Cutaneous Viral and Fungal Infections Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"You are covering a clinic and several patients with varicella are booked in. Which of these situations does the lecture say warrants prompt consultation?","choices":["Any patient who has previously received one dose of vaccine","Pregnancy, neonatal exposure, immunocompromise, or severe complications","A preadolescent child with a typical eruption and no fever","Any patient whose lesions have already crusted over completely"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — four named triggers for prompt consultation. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 68-year-old has a zoster eruption over the forehead and upper eyelid, with lesions on the tip of the nose. He reports eye pain and photophobia. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Arrange ophthalmology evaluation within two weeks and withhold antivirals until then","Start a topical antiviral eye ointment and review the patient in one week","Start a systemic corticosteroid alone and arrange same-day ophthalmology evaluation","Start systemic antiviral therapy immediately and arrange same-day ophthalmology evaluation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — do not delay the antiviral while awaiting the eye review. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A patient with acute herpes zoster asks how long he remains infectious. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["He was never infectious at any point","Until the lesions have dried","Until the pain has resolved completely","For 21 days from the onset of the rash"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — infectious until the lesions dry. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 31-year-old dental hygienist has a painful, swollen, erythematous index fingertip with grouped vesicles. She had burning and tingling in the finger the day before. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Contact dermatitis","Onychomycosis","Herpetic whitlow","Bacterial felon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a prodrome followed by grouped vesicles on a swollen digit. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy is brought in with an itchy scalp and a patch of hair loss. On examination there is a scaly grey ring-shaped patch with visible black dots where hairs have broken off at the surface, and there is tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Psoriasis","Tinea capitis","Alopecia areata","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — broken hairs, scale and lymphadenopathy in a preadolescent child. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man who uses a communal gym shower has itching between his toes. There is maceration with erythematous erosions between the third and fourth interspaces, with fissuring. Which variant of tinea pedis is this?","choices":["Vesiculobullous, which is the moist acute form with vesicles or bullae","Mosaic, which is a cluster of confluent lesions on weight-bearing surfaces","Interdigital, the most common form","Hyperkeratotic, which produces plantar erythema with diffuse thickening"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — maceration and erosion in the third and fourth interspaces. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A patient has an asymptomatic plantar wart found incidentally. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Salicylic acid 40% daily for twelve weeks in every patient found to have one","No therapy is required, since plantar warts need treatment only when painful","Cryotherapy every two to three weeks until the lesion has fully resolved","Immediate surgical excision to prevent it clustering into a mosaic wart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and many warts resolve spontaneously. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 145"},{"q":"A 24-year-old has a first episode of painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base on the vulva, which have broken down into shallow ulcers. She has fever, headache and tender inguinal nodes. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes simplex virus infection","Primary syphilis","Chancroid ulceration","Vulvovaginal candidiasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — grouped vesicles becoming shallow painful ulcers, with a systemic prodrome. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with obesity has a sore rash under both breasts and asks what intertrigo means and whether she has a yeast infection. What is intertrigo, and how does Candida relate to it?","choices":["A hypersensitivity reaction to a distant fungal infection, which appears in the body folds","An inflammatory rash caused by friction, moisture and heat trapped in body folds, which Candida may secondarily infect","A primary Candida infection of the body folds, which trapped friction, moisture and heat then worsen over time","A dermatophyte infection of the body folds, which yeast may secondarily infect once treated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — Candida is a secondary invader of an existing intertrigo. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A patient has pityriasis versicolor over the upper trunk. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Topical therapy, such as ketoconazole shampoo or cream, selenium sulfide, zinc pyrithione, ciclopirox, or topical terbinafine","Oral therapy, such as fluconazole or itraconazole, given to every patient at presentation whatever the extent of disease","Oral terbinafine, given for six weeks because it concentrates well in sweat","Topical nystatin, applied twice daily until the scale has fully resolved"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — topical therapy is first-line. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 30-year-old with extensive pityriasis versicolor asks about an oral tablet a friend was given. Which oral agent does the lecture say must NOT be used here, and why?","choices":["Oral itraconazole, because it achieves inadequate levels in sweat to clear the organism","Oral griseofulvin, because it has no activity against Malassezia species at any dose","Oral ketoconazole, because serious hepatic and adrenal toxicity outweighs the benefit in a superficial infection","Oral fluconazole, because serious hepatic and adrenal toxicity outweighs the benefit in superficial infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the risk-benefit balance fails for superficial disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A patient with a few small asymptomatic common warts asks for aggressive freezing to remove them quickly. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Aggressive freezing is appropriate, because common warts do not resolve without treatment","Avoid freezing entirely, because cryotherapy is never an appropriate wart treatment","Avoid excessive freezing or destructive therapy for benign lesions likely to resolve spontaneously","Aggressive freezing is appropriate, because it eradicates the virus and prevents recurrence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — weigh the harms against a self-limiting lesion. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 149"},{"q":"A patient asks why you will not prescribe the combination steroid and antifungal cream that a friend recommended. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Corticosteroids can mask and worsen a dermatophyte infection, producing tinea incognito","Combination products are less effective because the two drugs chemically inactivate each other","Combination products cannot be used on the trunk because of the risk of systemic absorption","Corticosteroids prevent the antifungal from reaching the stratum corneum where the fungus lives"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is exactly the mechanism of tinea incognito. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has fever and a generalized itchy eruption. On the trunk, scalp and face there are macules, papules, vesicles and crusts all present at the same time. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes zoster","Molluscum contagiosum","Herpes simplex","Varicella"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — several stages appearing simultaneously is the defining feature. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A patient has recurrent, extensive candidal intertrigo. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Evaluate for diabetes and immunosuppression","Repeat the same topical course for a further six months","Switch to a systemic corticosteroid to control the inflammation","Refer for surgical excision of the affected skin folds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — recurrent or extensive disease prompts this. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A patient with a confirmed id reaction asks what is causing the eruption on his hands. What is the proposed mechanism, and which primary infection is commonly responsible?","choices":["The mechanism is unknown, possibly a delayed-type hypersensitivity; tinea pedis is the common primary infection","The mechanism is direct fungal spread through the bloodstream; tinea capitis is the common primary infection","The mechanism is an immediate immunoglobulin E reaction; tinea cruris is the common primary infection","The mechanism is toxin-mediated; onychomycosis is the common primary infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck is explicit that the mechanism is unknown. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Culture from a child's scalp grows a Trichophyton species. Which oral agent does the lecture say is generally favored?","choices":["Selenium sulfide","Terbinafine","Griseofulvin","Ketoconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — terbinafine is generally favored for Trichophyton. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"An immunocompromised patient has had the first dose of recombinant zoster vaccine and needs faster completion. What interval is permitted for the second dose?","choices":["1 to 2 months after the first dose","2 to 6 months after the first dose, with no shortening permitted","6 to 12 months after the first dose","At least 12 months after the first dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a permitted shortening from the standard 2 to 6 months. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A mother asks why her 6-year-old daughter developed a scalp fungal infection when she herself has never had one. Which explanation best fits the epidemiology in the lecture?","choices":["It is predominantly a disease of preadolescent children, because the scalp skin is thinner before puberty and offers less of a mechanical barrier","It is predominantly a disease of preadolescent children, because school-age immunity to dermatophytes has not yet developed at that age","It is equally common at all ages, and the difference here is simply exposure at school rather than anything about the child","It is predominantly a disease of preadolescent children, because after puberty changes in the fatty acid content of sebum are believed to inhibit dermatophyte growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sebum fatty acids after puberty are the proposed reason. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A man has a localized tinea cruris plaque. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["A topical corticosteroid to relieve the itch, with an antifungal added only if it fails","A topical allylamine such as terbinafine, or a topical azole such as ketoconazole","An oral azole such as ketoconazole, given for a minimum of twelve weeks","Topical nystatin applied twice daily until the plaque has fully resolved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — oral therapy is reserved for extensive or refractory disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A patient with zoster has severe disseminated disease with visceral involvement. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["A systemic corticosteroid alone with close outpatient review","Topical antiviral therapy with cool compresses and calamine","Intravenous acyclovir with specialist or hospital management","Oral valacyclovir at standard dose as an outpatient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — also for central nervous system or sight-threatening disease. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A patient has an unusual, poorly defined, extensive eruption on the trunk. She has been applying a topical corticosteroid for months; it settles while she uses it and flares when she stops, prompting more steroid. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Psoriasis","An id reaction","Tinea incognito","Nummular eczema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — tinea whose appearance is altered by inappropriate steroid treatment. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A patient asks whether taking a corticosteroid during his shingles rash would have prevented his current neuralgia. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Corticosteroids prevent it and may be given in place of antiviral therapy in mild disease","Topical and systemic corticosteroids do not prevent postherpetic neuralgia and should never replace antiviral therapy","Topical and systemic corticosteroids reliably prevent postherpetic neuralgia when given within 72 hours","Systemic corticosteroids prevent it, though topical ones do not, so it should have been offered"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a common misconception the deck addresses directly. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A patient with postherpetic neuralgia needs pain control. Which is the most appropriate first-line option?","choices":["A long-acting opioid titrated to effect over several months, with a capsaicin patch","A systemic corticosteroid tapered over three months","Continued antiviral therapy at a suppressive dose","Gabapentin or pregabalin, an appropriate tricyclic antidepressant, or topical lidocaine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a capsaicin patch may also help. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with diabetes has thickened, discoloured, crumbling great toenails with subungual debris and separation of the nail from its bed. Which risk factors in his history does the lecture specifically link to this condition?","choices":["Obesity, incontinence, immobility, recent antibiotics, and immunosuppression","Heat, humidity, oily skin, sweating, and corticosteroid exposure","Sharing combs and hats, contact with pets, and exposure to fallen hairs","Tinea pedis, age, diabetes, trauma, occlusive footwear, psoriasis, and vascular disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — seven named risk factors. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A patient has a confirmed id reaction on the hands secondary to tinea pedis. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Treat the primary dermatophyte infection, since the id reaction resolves when it is treated","Treat the hand eruption with a topical antifungal, since fungus is present at both sites","Treat the hand eruption with an oral antibiotic, since the reaction is bacterial in origin","Observe both sites without treatment, since the reaction and the primary infection both self-resolve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the distant eruption follows the primary site. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man is diagnosed with tinea barbae. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Topical antifungal cream alone applied twice daily to the beard area, plus shaving or hair removal and warm compresses to remove crusts","Oral antibiotic therapy with a first-generation cephalosporin, plus shaving and warm compresses","Topical corticosteroid to settle the inflammation first, followed by an oral antifungal after two weeks","Oral antifungal therapy with griseofulvin or terbinafine, plus shaving or hair removal and warm compresses to remove crusts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — topicals do not penetrate the follicle. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A patient is diagnosed with tinea manuum affecting one palm. Which is the most appropriate treatment approach?","choices":["The same as for tinea pedis: topical for localized disease, oral for extensive, recurrent, refractory or immunocompromised disease","The same as for tinea capitis: oral therapy in every patient with hand disease, because topical agents cannot penetrate the affected skin","The same as for candidal intertrigo: topical nystatin with correction of the local environment","The same as for pityriasis versicolor: selenium sulfide lotion applied daily for seven days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck routes tinea manuum to the tinea pedis approach. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"The parents of a child with molluscum contagiosum want it treated immediately. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Immediate destruction of every lesion is required, because untreated molluscum progresses to malignancy","Observation is appropriate, because the lesions always resolve within two to three weeks without treatment","Immediate oral antiviral therapy is required, because the lesions will otherwise spread to the whole family","Observation is appropriate for many patients, because most clear spontaneously and procedures may blister, pigment, or scar"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — although resolution may take months to years. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"A 30-year-old runner has a painful plantar wart on the ball of the foot. Which treatments does the lecture give?","choices":["Berdazimer 10.3% gel or a topical retinoid","Oral terbinafine or topical ciclopirox","Salicylic acid 40% or cryotherapy","Cantharidin 0.7% or curettage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the two named options for plantar warts. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 146"}]};
